<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-integrals-of-trig-functions" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Integrals of Trigonometric Functions</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>In this section, we explore the integrals (antiderivatives) of various trigonometric functions. First, recall the antiderivative of sine and cosine,</p>
    <md>
      <mrow>\int \sin{x} \,dx \amp= -\cos{x} + C</mrow>
      <mrow>\text{and} \qquad \int \cos{x} \,dx \amp= \sin{x} + C</mrow>
    </md>
    <p>The integrals of the other trigonometric functions are somewhat more complicated, and require using substitution. First, here are all of them together,</p>
    <me>\boxed{\begin{array}{ll}
      \int \sin{x} \,dx = -\cos{x} + C \amp \int \cos{x} \,dx = \sin{x} + C \\
      \int \sec{x} \,dx = \ln{\abs{\sec{x} + \tan{x}}} + C \amp \int \csc{x} \,dx = -\ln{\abs{\csc{x} + \cot{x}}} + C \\
      \int \tan{x} \,dx = -\ln{\abs{\cos{x}}} + C \amp \int \cot{x} \,dx = \ln{\abs{\sin{x}}} + C
    \end{array}}</me>
    <p>You may get a formula sheet on your final exam with these formulas, or you may be expected to memorize them (or derive them if needed).</p>

    <remark>
      <p>A pattern that can help you memorize these antiderivatives, is that the antiderivative of the <q>co</q> functions (cosine, cosecant, cotangent) are similar to their corresponding non-co functions (sine, secant, tangent), except,</p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>They have the <em>opposite sign</em> (so if the non-co function has a positive sign, the co function has a negative sign, and vice versa).</p></li>
        <li>
          <p>They have similar function parts, but <q>flipped</q>, in that non-co functions become co functions, and co becomes non-co. In particular,</p>
          <tabular halign="left">
            <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
              <cell>integral of...</cell>
              <cell><alert>non-co-function</alert></cell>
              <cell></cell>
              <cell><alert>co-function</alert></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell></cell>
              <cell><m>\cos{x}</m></cell>
              <cell><m>\longleftrightarrow</m></cell>
              <cell><m>\sin{x}</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell></cell>
              <cell><m>\ln{\abs{\sec{x}+\tan{x}}}</m></cell>
              <cell><m>\longleftrightarrow</m></cell>
              <cell><m>\ln{\abs{\csc{x}+\cot{x}}}</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell></cell>
              <cell><m>\ln{\abs{\cos{x}}}</m></cell>
              <cell><m>\longleftrightarrow</m></cell>
              <cell><m>\ln{\abs{\sin{x}}}</m></cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </remark>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-integrals-tan-sec-csc-cot">
    <title>Integrals of Tangent, Secant, Cosecant, and Cotangent</title>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-integral-tangent">
      <title>Integral of Tangent</title>
      <statement>
        <me>\boxed{\begin{array}{cc}
          \int \tan{x} \,dx \amp= -\ln{\abs{\cos{x}}} + C \\
          \amp= \ln{\abs{\sec{x}}} + C
        \end{array}}</me>
      </statement>
    </theorem>

    <remark>
      <p>Note that these two antiderivatives are equivalent, because,</p>
      <md>
        <mrow>-\ln{\abs{\cos{x}}} \amp= \ln{\brac{\abs{\cos{x}}^{-1}}} \amp\amp\text{by log laws}</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= \ln{\abs{\frac{1}{\cos{x}}}}</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= \ln{\abs{\sec{x}}}</mrow>
      </md>
    </remark>

    <example>
      <title>Deriving the Integral of Tangent</title>
      <p>To integrate <m>\tan{x}</m>, recall that <m>\tan{x} = \frac{\sin{x}}{\cos{x}}</m>. We can rewrite it as <m>\frac{\sin{x}}{\cos{x}}</m>, and use substitution,</p>
      <me>\int \tan{x} \,dx = \int \frac{\sin{x}}{\cos{x}} \,dx</me>
      <p>Notice that there is <m>\cos{x}</m> in the denominator, and its derivative <m>-\sin{x}</m> is in the numerator (except with a negative sign), so we can use substitution. Then,</p>
      <md>
        <mrow>u = \cos{x} \qquad du \amp= -\sin{x} \,dx</mrow>
        <mrow>-du \amp= \sin{x} \,dx</mrow>
      </md>
      <p>Then,</p>
      <md>
        <mrow>\amp= -\int \frac{1}{u} \,du</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= -\ln{\abs{u}} + C</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= -\ln{\abs{\cos{x}}} + C</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= \ln{\abs{(\cos{x})^{-1}}} + C</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= \ln{\abs{\sec{x}}} + C</mrow>
      </md>
    </example>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-integral-secant">
      <title>Integral of Secant</title>
      <statement>
        <me>\boxed{\int \sec{x} \,dx = \ln{\abs{\sec{x} + \tan{x}}} + C}</me>
      </statement>
    </theorem>

    <example>
      <title>Deriving the Integral of Secant</title>
      <p>There are multiple ways to integrate <m>\sec{x}</m>, but the most common way is to use a very special trick, of multiplying the numerator and denominator by <m>\sec{x} + \tan{x}</m>,</p>
      <me>\int \sec{x} \,dx = \int \frac{\sec{x}(\sec{x} + \tan{x})}{\sec{x} + \tan{x}} \,dx</me>
      <p>It turns out that this allows us to use substitution, because there is <m>\sec{x}+\tan{x}</m> in the denominator, and its derivative is <m>\sec{x} \tan{x} + \sec^2{x}</m>, which is exactly what is in the numerator. Then,</p>
      <md>
        <mrow>u = \sec{x} + \tan{x} \qquad du \amp= (\sec{x} \tan{x} + \sec^2{x}) \,dx</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= \sec{x}(\sec{x} + \tan{x}) \,dx</mrow>
      </md>
      <p>Then,</p>
      <md>
        <mrow>\amp= \int \frac{1}{u} \,du</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= \ln{\abs{u}} + C</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= \ln{\abs{\sec{x} + \tan{x}}} + C</mrow>
      </md>
    </example>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-integral-cosecant">
      <title>Integral of Cosecant</title>
      <statement>
        <me>\boxed{\int \csc{x} \,dx = -\ln{\abs{\csc{x} + \cot{x}}} + C}</me>
      </statement>
    </theorem>

    <example>
      <title>Deriving the Integral of Cosecant</title>
      <p>To integrate <m>\csc{x}</m>, we can use the same trick as secant, but this time multiplying the numerator and denominator by <m>\csc{x} + \cot{x}</m>,</p>
      <me>\int \csc{x} \,dx = \int \frac{\csc{x}(\csc{x} + \cot{x})}{\csc{x} + \cot{x}} \,dx</me>
      <p>Again, this allows us to use substitution, because there is <m>\csc{x} + \cot{x}</m> in the denominator, and its derivative is <m>-\csc{x} \cot{x} - \csc^2{x}</m>, which is what is in the numerator (except with a negative sign). Then,</p>
      <md>
        <mrow>u = \csc{x} + \cot{x} \qquad du \amp= -\csc{x} \cot{x} - \csc^2{x} \,dx</mrow>
        <mrow>-du \amp= \csc{x} \cot{x} + \csc^2{x} \,dx</mrow>
      </md>
      <p>Then,</p>
      <md>
        <mrow>\amp= -\int \frac{1}{u} \,du</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= -\ln{\abs{u}} + C</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= -\ln{\abs{\csc{x} + \cot{x}}} + C</mrow>
      </md>
    </example>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-integral-cotangent">
      <title>Integral of Cotangent</title>
      <statement>
        <me>\boxed{\begin{array}{cc}
          \int \cot{x} \,dx \amp= \ln{\abs{\sin{x}}} + C \\
          \amp= -\ln{\abs{\csc{x}}} + C
        \end{array}}</me>
      </statement>
    </theorem>

    <remark>
      <p>Note that these two antiderivatives are equivalent, because,</p>
      <md>
        <mrow>\ln{\abs{\csc{x}}} \amp= \ln{\abs{\frac{1}{\sin{x}}}} \amp\amp\text{by definition of cosecant}</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= \ln{\abs{\sin{x}}^{-1}}</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= -\ln{\abs{\sin{x}}} \amp\amp\text{by log laws}</mrow>
      </md>
    </remark>

    <example>
      <title>Deriving the Integral of Cotangent</title>
      <p>To integrate <m>\cot{x}</m>, it is similar to <m>\tan{x}</m>. We can rewrite it as <m>\frac{\cos{x}}{\sin{x}}</m>, and use substitution,</p>
      <me>\int \cot{x} \,dx = \int \frac{\cos{x}}{\sin{x}} \,dx</me>
      <p>Then,</p>
      <md>
        <mrow>u = \sin{x} \qquad du \amp= \cos{x} \,dx</mrow>
      </md>
      <p>Then,</p>
      <md>
        <mrow>\amp= \int \frac{1}{u} \,du</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= \ln{\abs{u}} + C</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= \ln{\abs{\sin{x}}} + C</mrow>
      </md>
    </example>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-integrals-sin2-cos2">
    <title>Integral of <m>\sin^2{x}</m> and <m>\cos^2{x}</m></title>

    <p>Two important trig integrals that come up often are <m>\int \sin^2{x} \,dx</m> and <m>\int \cos^2{x} \,dx</m>.</p>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-integrals-sin2-cos2">
      <title>Integrals of Squared Sine and Cosine</title>
      <statement>
        <me>\boxed{\begin{array}{rl} \int \cos^2{x} \,dx \amp= \frac{1}{2} x + \frac{1}{4} \sin{(2x)} + C \\[5pt]
        \amp= \frac{1}{2}x + \frac{1}{2}\sin{x} \cos{x} + C \\[5pt]
        \int \sin^2{x} \,dx \amp= \frac{1}{2} x - \frac{1}{4} \sin{(2x)} + C \\[5pt]
        \amp= \frac{1}{2}x - \frac{1}{2}\sin{x} \cos{x} + C \end{array}}</me>
      </statement>
    </theorem>

    <p>These integrals show up often enough that they are helpful to memorize, or at least memorize the method of deriving them.</p>

    <example>
      <title>Integral of <m>\cos^2{x}</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>Consider,</p>
        <me>\int \cos^2{x} \,dx</me>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>Unfortunately, there is no simple substitution that can be used to evaluate this integral (like, for example, <m>u=\cos{x}</m>). Also, you can't just integrate <m>\cos^2{x}</m> as if it were 2 <m>\cos{x}</m> functions multiplied together,,</p>
        <md>
          <mrow>\int \cos^2{x} \,dx \amp\neq \int \cos{x} \,dx \cdot \int \cos{x} \,dx</mrow>
          <mrow>\int \cos^2{x} \,dx \amp\neq \sin^2{x} + C</mrow>
        </md>
        <p>Instead, we have to use a particular trigonometric identity, called the power-reducing identities. There are 2 of them, one for cosine and one for sine,</p>
        <me>\cos^2{x} = \frac{1+\cos{(2x)}}{2} \qquad \text{and} \qquad \sin^2{x} = \frac{1-\cos{(2x)}}{2}</me>
        <p>As their name suggests, these identities express <m>\cos^2{x}</m> and <m>\sin^2{x}</m> (cosine and sine to the 2nd power) in terms of <m>\cos{(2x)}</m>, which is to the 1st power.</p>
        <p>These identities can be derived from the double-angle identities for cosine,</p>
        <md>
          <mrow>\cos{(2x)} \amp= 2\cos^2{x} - 1</mrow>
          <mrow>\cos{(2x)} \amp= 1 - 2\sin^2{x}</mrow>
        </md>
        <p>If you solve the first equation to isolate for <m>\cos^2{x}</m>, and the second equation for <m>\sin^2{x}</m>, you get the power-reducing identities.</p>
        <p>Using these identities, we can evaluate the integral of <m>\cos^2{x}</m>,</p>
        <md>
          <mrow>\int \cos^2{x} \,dx \amp= \int \frac{1 + \cos{(2x)}}{2} \,dx</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{2} \int (1 + \cos{(2x)}) \,dx</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{2} \brac{x + \frac{1}{2} \sin{(2x)}} + C</mrow>
        </md>
        <p>Further, the double-angle identity <m>\sin{(2x)} = 2 \sin{x} \cos{x}</m> can be used to rewrite the antiderivative in terms of only single angles <m>x</m>,</p>
        <md>
          <mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{2} x + \frac{1}{2} \sin{x} \cos{x} + C</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{2}(x + \sin{x} \cos{x}) + C</mrow>
        </md>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <p>The integral of <m>\sin^2{x}</m> can be evaluated in the same way, using the other power-reducing identity,</p>

    <example>
      <title>Integral of <m>\sin^2{x}</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>Consider the integral,</p>
        <me>\int \sin^2{x} \,dx</me>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>Use the power-reducing identity for sine <m>\sin^2{x} = \frac{1-\cos{(2x)}}{2}</m>,</p>
        <md>
          <mrow>\int \sin^2{x} \,dx \amp= \int \frac{1 - \cos{(2x)}}{2} \,dx</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{2} \int (1 - \cos{(2x)}) \,dx</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{2} \brac{x - \frac{1}{2} \sin{(2x)}} + C</mrow>
        </md>
        <p>Again, we can use the double-angle identity <m>\sin{(2x)} = 2 \sin{x} \cos{x}</m> to rewrite the antiderivative in terms of only single angles <m>x</m>,</p>
        <md>
          <mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{2} x - \frac{1}{2} \sin{x} \cos{x} + C</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{2}(x - \sin{x} \cos{x}) + C</mrow>
        </md>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <p>For reference, here are the power-reducing identities again,</p>
    <me>\boxed{\begin{array}{rl} \cos^2{x} \amp= \frac{1+\cos{(2x)}}{2} \\[5pt]
    \sin^2{x} \amp= \frac{1-\cos{(2x)}}{2} \end{array}}</me>
    <p>You may get these identities on your formula sheet for your exams, or you may need to memorize them.</p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-trig-integrals-examples">
    <title>Examples</title>

    <project>
      <title>Basic Trigonometric Integrals Practice</title>
      <introduction><p>Evaluate each integral.</p></introduction>
      <task><statement><p><m>\int (\sec{x}+1)^2 \,dx</m></p></statement><hint><p>Expand, and use the formula for <m>\int \sec^2{x} \,dx</m>.</p></hint><answer><p><m>\tan{x} + 2\ln{\abs{\sec{x} + \tan{x}}} + x + C</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>\int \frac{1}{1-\sin{x}} \,dx</m></p></statement><hint><p>Multiply the numerator and denominator by <m>1+\sin{x}</m>, and split the fraction.</p></hint><answer><p><m>\tan{x} + \sec{x} + C</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>\int \frac{1+\sin{x}}{\cos^2{x}} \,dx</m></p></statement><hint><p>Split the fraction into <m>\sec^2{x} + \sec{x}\tan{x}</m>.</p></hint><answer><p><m>\tan{x} + \sec{x} + C</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>\int (\sec{x} - \tan{x})^2 \,dx</m></p></statement><hint><p>Expand, and use <m>\tan^2{x} = \sec^2{x} - 1</m>.</p></hint><answer><p><m>2\tan{x} - 2\sec{x} - x + C</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>\int (\csc{x} - \sec{x})(\sin{x} + \cos{x}) \,dx</m></p></statement><hint><p>Distribute; use <m>\csc{x} \sin{x} = 1</m> and <m>\sec{x} \cos{x} = 1</m>.</p></hint><answer><p><m>\ln{\abs{\sin{x}}} + \ln{\abs{\cos{x}}} + C</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>\int_{\pi/4}^{\pi/3} \frac{dx}{\cos^2{x}\tan{x}}</m></p></statement><hint><p>Rewrite as <m>\int \frac{\sec^2{x}}{\tan{x}} \,dx</m> and use <m>u = \tan{x}</m>.</p></hint><answer><p><m>\frac{1}{2}\ln{3}</m></p></answer></task>
    </project>

    <project>
      <title>More Trigonometric Integrals Practice</title>
      <introduction><p>Evaluate each integral.</p></introduction>
      <task><statement><p><m>\int \frac{dx}{\cos{x} - 1}</m></p></statement><hint><p>Multiply numerator and denominator by <m>\cos{x} + 1</m>, and use <m>\cos^2{x} - 1 = -\sin^2{x}</m>.</p></hint><answer><p><m>\csc{x} + \cot{x} + C</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>\int \frac{dx}{\sec{x} + \tan{x}}</m></p></statement><hint><p>Multiply numerator and denominator by <m>\sec{x} - \tan{x}</m>, and use <m>\sec^2{x} - \tan^2{x} = 1</m>.</p></hint><answer><p><m>\ln{\abs{1 + \sin{x}}} + C</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>\int (\cos{x}\sin{2x} + \sin{x}\cos{2x}) \,dx</m></p></statement><hint><p>Use the sine addition formula <m>\sin{(A+B)} = \sin{A}\cos{B} + \cos{A}\sin{B}</m>.</p></hint><answer><p><m>-\frac{1}{3}\cos{3x} + C</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>\int \frac{dx}{1 - \sec{x}}</m></p></statement><hint><p>Rewrite as <m>\int \frac{\cos{x}}{\cos{x} - 1} \,dx</m>, then multiply by the conjugate <m>\frac{\cos{x}+1}{\cos{x}+1}</m>.</p></hint><answer><p><m>\cot{x} + \csc{x} + x + C</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>\int (\sec{x} + \cot{x})^2 \,dx</m></p></statement><hint><p>Expand, and use <m>\cot^2{x} = \csc^2{x} - 1</m> and <m>\sec{x}\cot{x} = \csc{x}</m>.</p></hint><answer><p><m>\tan{x} - 2\ln{\abs{\csc{x} + \cot{x}}} - \cot{x} - x + C</m></p></answer></task>
    </project>
  </subsection>

</section>
